---
permalink: /admin/analytics/
title: "Analytics | Admin"
bodyScripts:
  - src: "/scripts/admin-analytics.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/contact-widget.js"
    type: "module"
  - src: "/scripts/palette.js"
    type: "module"
---
<section class="app-page">
  <div class="app-prose">

  {% include "chrome/admin-sections.njk" %}

  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-2">Analytics</h1>
  <p class="text-brand-muted mb-8 max-w-prose">
    Who reads the public site, what they read, and how they got there, and the same for the app.
    Counted on my own server: a row holds a path, which of the two sites it was on,
    the host a reader arrived from and the time. No address, no cookie, no identifier of any kind.
  </p>

  {# The one control. It changes `days`, and everything below is drawn from the one answer that
     comes back - so there is nothing on this page that can be showing a different window from
     anything else on it. #}
  <div class="mb-6 flex items-center gap-2">
    <label for="range" class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Last</label>
    <select id="range" class="field w-auto py-1.5 text-sm">
      <option value="7">7 days</option>
      <option value="30" selected>30 days</option>
      <option value="90">90 days</option>
      <option value="365">365 days</option>
    </select>
  </div>

  <div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="mb-6 text-sm"></div>

  {# Every figure starts as an em dash marked `data-loading`, never as a zero. A zero is a
     measurement and these have not been measured yet; the dash is the page saying it does not
     know. The same rule is what the empty window does below: "nothing yet" is an answer, and a
     nought drawn where no counting has happened is an invention. #}
  <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3 mb-10">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Views</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="views" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="viewsNote">In this window.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Against the window before</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="change" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="changeNote">The equally long window before this one.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">From LinkedIn</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="linkedin" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="linkedinNote">Views whose referrer was a LinkedIn domain.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Views per day</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    Every day in the range, including the ones with nothing on them.
  </p>
  <div id="chart" class="card mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Which pages are read</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    Split by property, because "which of the two sites" is the first question and a merged list
    of paths cannot answer it.
  </p>
  <div id="paths" class="mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Where readers came from</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    The host only. A full referring URL can carry somebody's search terms in its query string, so
    the API keeps the host and throws the rest away before anything is written down.
  </p>
  <div id="referrers">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

</div>

</section>
